one convention for converting the magnitude of the \(\textit{BF}\) to a discrete decision about the models is that there is “substantial” evidence for model \(m = 1\) when the \(\textit{BF}\) exceeds \(3.0\) and, equivalently, “substantial” evidence for model \(m = 2\) when the \(\textit{BF}\) is less than \(1/3\) (Jeffreys, 1961; Kass & Raftery, 1995; Wetzels et al., 2011). (p. 268)
